AB  - The rise of Large Language Models (LLMs) has introduced new opportunities and challenges for Software Engineering Education (SEE), particularly through their emergent ability to assist with code generation and problem solving. While traditional Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) have long aimed to provide personalized learning support, they typically require significant manual authoring and struggle to handle the openended nature of programming tasks. Building on previous work exploring ITS, this paper presents a novel platform that leverages LLMs to offer students continuous, personalized SEE support through railroaded, prompt-tuned interactions. In addition to the always-available tutoring interface, the platform provides teachers with a collaborative environment where they can monitor student progress, review interactions, and contribute instructional prompts. The system architecture was developed using a designbased research methodology and informed by field studies with programming instructors and students. Initial use of the platform suggests potential to support engagement, autonomous problemsolving, and collaborative teaching practices.
