AB  - Creative coding with p5.js suffers from template reCreative coding with p5.js suffers from template reuse, inefficient debugging, and weak creative control. We present a Computational Thinking (CT)–aligned GPT-5–p5.js pipeline that maps natural-language intentions to executable code via four phases — Prompt, Plan, Program, and Polish — embedding task decomposition, abstraction, and algorithmic logic to ensure traceable semantics-to-code mapping. Across 30 tasks spanning basic to advanced levels, the GPT-assisted group reduced completion time by 45% versus manual coding. Code quality improved with a 96% first-run executability rate and a 32% lower error density, without increasing cyclomatic complexity. Usability rose markedly: System Usability Scale scores averaged 82.5 compared with 64.7 for controls (p < 0.001), alongside higher task success. Creative performance increased by 16% in expert ratings and by a higher visual-diversity index, with gains amplifying on harder tasks and among novices. These results indicate that CT integration with LLMs yields a transparent, efficient, and controllable workflow for interactive art generation, and artifacts are released for replication. The paper has been accepted for publication.
