AB  - Many modern large-language models (LLMs) achieve high accuracy on complex reasoning benchmarks, but their multi-step inference processes remain largely opaque, making it difficult to trust or debug model outputs. Chain-of-thought prompting improves performance by exposing intermediate reasoning steps, yet single-chain methods can still propagate errors and require extensive annotated data. To address these challenges, we propose a collaborative multi-agent Chain-of-Thought architecture that integrates three complementary reasoning chains—Graph of Thoughts, Layer of Thoughts, and Reversal of Thoughts—allowing them to engage in structured debate and synthesis. Our system generates both a transparent "ThoughtFlow" and a final answer, then applies an Implicit Process Reward Model to iteratively refine these outputs without additional annotations. Evaluation on standard benchmarks demonstrates that our approach not only matches or exceeds state-of-the-art accuracy but also significantly enhances reasoning interpretability and reduces reliance on large labeled datasets.
