AB  - This paper addresses a critical deficiency in AI-assisted Specification-Driven Development (SDD). We propose and empirically evaluate an enhanced SDD workflow that integrates LLM-generated transition diagrams with agent skills as pivotal intermediaries. This workflow serves a dual purpose: steering system code generation and enabling automated verification. Regarding generation control, an evaluation across 15 cases demonstrates that this dual-model workflow yields a statistically significant increase in average feature completeness, rising from 87.28% to 95.43%. Notably, these functional gains were achieved without inflating code scale, cyclomatic complexity, or cognitive complexity. Regarding automated verification, we compare 17 distinct path-generation prompt strategies for test case synthesis. Our analysis reveals that while requirement coverage shows marginal variance across strategies, transition coverage exhibits significantly higher discriminative power. Consequently, we suggest evaluating the cost-effectiveness of the strategies by analyzing the trade-off between transition coverage and execution overhead through Pareto filtering. Overall, this study demonstrates that incorporating state-transition modeling into SDD not only enhances the functional integrity of synthesized code but also provides a more discriminative verification baseline than traditional requirement-based metrics, ultimately enabling the automated and cost-optimized synthesis of high-coverage test suites.
