AB  - Heuristics are widely used for dealing with complex Combinatorial Optimization Problems (COPs). However, traditional Automated Heuristic Design (AHD) methods using Large Language Models (LLMs) can be often very rigid and require static prompt templates. This paper proposes Efficient Heuristic Strategy Evolution (EEHS), a novel evolutionary paradigm that leverages collaborative Strategist and Worker LLMs. EEHS rep resents the generation process in two parts: “State-Aware Meta Operator Generation” and “Instruction-Guided Code Generation.” The strategies are described as adaptive meta-operators Mt by a high-level Strategist using evolutionary states. They are then translated into executable codes by a low-level Worker following the constraints of Mt. The evolution of these instructions and a hybrid selection mechanism makes it very effective for avoiding the rigidity of static paradigms. Experiments on Online Bin Packing, the Traveling Salesman Problem, and the Flow Shop Scheduling Problem demonstrate that EEHS outperforms state of-the-art methods. Particularly, EEHS significantly reduces the evaluation overhead compared to EoH, the representative LLM based benchmark. This reduction leads to a 2.9× to 6.9× speedup and decreases LLM inference costs by 75%, providing a practical paradigm for automated algorithm design.
