AB  - Over the last several years enterprise software development has experienced dramatic changes, evolving from static automated development processes to a substantially more dynamic process that is managed by agents orchestrated by Generative AI. At the core of this evolution has been an open standards-based environment that provides an extensive number of capabilities referred to as Model Context Protocol (MCP). One purpose of the Model Context Protocol (MCP) is to enable consistent interaction with large language models (LLMs) regardless of the variability in the types of data sources, tools, and workflows present within the current Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC). The purpose of this work is to provide a more detailed view of about how to use an MCP to facilitate the orchestration of specialized AI agents across complex enterprise environments, specifically within Java-based ecosystems. In addition, this paper will also describe how JSON-RPC 2.0 is used in an architectural context to provide bi-directional communication between the various functional components of an MCP using standardized primitives (Resources, Prompts, and Tools) that enable AI agents to go beyond producing text-based content to taking action. The diverse agent-based secure development lifecycle (SDL) introduced in this document will include the following critical tasks: 1) Automated code reviews made possible through SonarQube integration, 2) Context-aware test generation enabled by Testkube, 3) Automated generation of release notes via a git-based ReNo tool, and 4) Cloud Native Deployment Scripting to Kubernetes. This paper provides details on handoff patterns analysis supported by state transfer mechanisms and token-based orchestration that support mitigation of the historical "N×M" integration bottleneck replaced by fragmented vendor-specific connectors with Universal "USB-C" Interface through MCP. It moves further into the socio-technical narrative of Human-AI collaboration for security governance using OAuth 2.1, together with the Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) quandaries involved in debugging Multi-Agent Swarms, thus expediting release velocity improvement over software quality enhancement and bringing forth a scalable foundation for next-generation best practices of AI-augmented software engineering.
