AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to generate unit tests and other test code from source code and natural-language prompts. This position paper examines why current standalone LLM-based test generation remains unsafe when generated tests are accepted without independent oracles or human review. Grounded in prior empirical studies and established testing criteria, it identifies six empirically reported or evaluation-derived failure modes across unit testing, mutation testing, integration testing, and fuzzing. We summarize the evidence in a compact matrix and use three short examples to show how generated tests can encode an implementation bug, repair away a failing bug-revealing candidate, or raise coverage through mocks without checking behavior. For each mode, we state a practical rule for safer LLM-assisted testing workflows.
