AB  - The development of artificial intelligence (AI) has facilitated performant network planning by relying on neural models to identify the complex patterns that lie in networks and the demand side. However, the resulting AI models are usually with fixed neural architectures or hyperparameter configurations, which would lead to fluctuating performances when they are applied to different network topologies or planning tasks. This paper proposes the first large-language-model (LLM)-driven automated machine learning (autoML) framework for network planning tasks, named LLMPlan. We fully exploit LLMs’ creative code generation abilities, pretrained knowledge on machine learning, and strong in-context learning abilities to automatically generate and optimize model configurations when network topology or network tasks change. Specifically, a plan-and-execute framework is designed to decompose the network planning task first and then compose codes for each decomposed task, which accelerates the process of generating implementable neural models. We then propose a novel LLM-based approach for hyperparameter optimization, which fine-tunes the LLM to equip it with fine-grained domain knowledge so as to search for performant models on network planning more efficiently. Extensive experiments on real-world networks exhibit that the normalized planning costs can be efficiently reduced by 16.26% ∼ 59.68% compared with state-of-the-art benchmarks. At least of 53.36% inference time can be further saved due to our high-performant neural network modeling with little overheads of solution refinement.
ER  - 

