AB  - With the rise of deep learning applications, numerous fuzzing tools have emerged to improve their security. However, traditional fuzzers frequently produce test cases that violate constraints and result in code branch loss. LLM-based fuzzers often lack effective guidance and high-quality training datasets, which makes it difficult for test cases to meet the standards used in fuzzing. This article introduces ALF, a new fuzzing method designed for deep learning libraries that integrates Few-Shot Learning (FSL) with the advanced code generation capabilities of LLMs. ALF autonomously generates high-quality test cases, which effectively solves the limitations of traditional fuzzing methods, especially in terms of test case diversity and semantic validity. At the same time, by adopting an adaptive feedback scheduling algorithm based on the multi-armed bandit method, ALF dynamically adjusts its test case generation strategy based on real-time execution coverage data. Comprehensive experimental evaluations reveal that ALF enhances the syntax pass rate by at least 10 % in the PyTorch library and achieves a 20 % increase in code coverage with fewer mutations compared to traditional fuzz testing techniques. In the TensorFlow library, coverage improves by over 25 %. These results underscore ALF's effectiveness in generating valid and diverse test cases for deep learning libraries while highlighting the challenges of utilizing LLMs in AI framework testing, such as knowledge gaps, the randomness of outputs, and the need for precise prompt engineering.
ER  - 

