AB  - The creation of individualized and contextualized digital learning material has, until recently, been a laborious and expensive undertaking limited to educators with technical competence. LLMs (Large Language Models) are changing this, as code can now be easily generated by non-experts. This study therefore explored the possibilities of creating interactive digital learning materials with LLMs in a case related to learning English as a second language. We found that LLMs could successfully generate fully working learning apps on a variety of topics, employing several didactic devices. However, it seems difficult to formulate prompts and debug problems without technical knowledge. We thus argue for the use of prompt templates that can serve as a starting point, inspiration, and learning resource for educators. We conclude that LLMs provide untapped potential to rapidly and effectively respond to students’ specific learning needs.
