AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) tend to exhibit "hallucinations" in tasks involving complex reasoning and calculations, often providing incorrect answers. To address this issue, we propose an innovative method called the Structured Reasoning Framework (SRF), which aims to enhance the reasoning performance of LLMs by incorporating code generation and Chain of Thought (CoT) mechanisms. SRF employs a multi-agent collaborative strategy that breaks down complex tasks into actionable steps, significantly improving the model’s accuracy and robustness. Experimental validation on multiple datasets demonstrates that SRF achieves outstanding performance improvements in reasoning accuracy, logical consistency, and richness. In the GPT-4o mini model, the overall score improved by 10.99% compared to the baseline, with accuracy and logic scores increasing by 22.5% and 11.0%, respectively. Enhancements from SRF are particularly notable in smaller-scale models (such as GPT-3.5), with the overall performance score improving by 23.79% over the baseline.
ER  - 

