AB  - Iterative refinement with large language models (LLMs) has become the dominant paradigm for code generation, yet its security dynamics remain poorly understood. We identify an iterative refinement paradox: specification drift during multi-objective optimization leads to gradual security degradation. Experiments on GPT-4o show that 43.7% of refinement chains contain more vulnerabilities than the baseline after 10 rounds, with cross-model validation confirming its pervasiveness. We further demonstrate that SAST-based gating fails to suppress degradation; instead, it increases the latent security degradation rate from 12.5% to 20.8%, as static rules miss structural degradations such as deleted defensive logic and weakened exception handling. To address these issues, we propose SCAFFOLD-CEGIS, a CEGIS-inspired multi-agent framework that converts implicit security prompts into explicit, verifiable constraints through semantic anchors, four-layer gated verification, and failure assimilation. The full framework reduces the latent degradation rate to $\mathbf{2. 1 \%}$ and achieves $\mathbf{1 0 0 \%}$ security monotonicity across six baselines while preserving code evolution capability.
ER  - 

